SUMMARY:Adaptive Ceramic Histories: Opening Reception and Curator Talk
DESCRIPTION:Adaptive Ceramic Histories: Opening Reception and Curator Talk            \n                                    \n                                \n                        \n                                                \n                                                        Free Registration                        \n                                            \n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n			\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Friday November 20\, 2026\nFree with registration \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    5:30 pm: Doors Open\n6:00 pm: Curator Talk & Dialogue\n7:00 pm: Reception \nJoin us to celebrate the opening of the exhibition Adaptive Ceramic Histories with a free curator talk and dialogue with Gardiner Museum Curatorial Resident Alexandra Box\, followed by a reception. Box will discuss her research on histories of medical and adaptive ceramics\, and how ceramics can guide our thinking and rethinking of sick and disabled people’s material conditions. Following her talk\, Box will be in conversation with Senior Curator\, Dr. Karine Tsoumis\, and Sofia Flores-Ledesma\, Programs & Education Coordinator. \nThe Gardiner Museum Curatorial Residency is made possible through the generous support of the Rebanks Family. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n			\n\n        \n          \n          \n                        \n               \n                General : Free\n                                        \n                        \n                                        \n                      \n\n                    \n          \n            \n  \n            \n                                Register Now\n              \n                        \n           \n                \n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                \n          \n          \n        \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    About the Exhibition \nAdaptive Ceramic Histories presents an expansive way of looking at histories of medical and adaptive ceramics created for sick or disabled users. The exhibition invites you to delve into the design of care from the vantage point of users\, departing from traditional histories of medicine centering on medical professionals. Instead\, it considers the possibilities of pleasure and metaphor to build a more fulsome knowledge of patient care to craft the future.\nLearn more \n  \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    About the Curator\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Alexandra Box\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Alexandra Box is an arts professional and artist. She has been awarded fellowships and artist residencies through Medalta Potteries\, Salt Spring Arts Council\, and Point Gallery. Box is a curatorial participant in the Knowledge Incubator & Mentorship Initiative through Redefine Arts. Her writing has been published by Hearth\, Peripheral Review\, and The Capilano Review. Box serves on the Board at Forest City Gallery and is a contributor to the Media Collaboratory. She holds a BFA (2020) in ceramics and curatorial practices from Emily Carr University and an MA (2024) in Art History from the University of Toronto. \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/adaptive-ceramic-histories-opening-reception-and-curator-talk/
